1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,910
BookLeaf's brief: thousands of author questions, all answered by hand.

2
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:13,330
Build an author portal, an admin portal, a REST API, and AI that sorts and drafts replies.

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:17,750
I built BookLeaf's new author support system.

4
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:19,870
Here's how, and why.

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:21,990
The obvious move?

6
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:24,610
Bolt on AI to type replies faster.

7
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:28,490
But a confident wrong number is worse than a slow reply.

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:31,590
So I reframed it.

9
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:34,350
Correct answers in front of a human, fast.

10
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:35,950
Money issues first.

11
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:38,850
And a desk that never stops when the AI does.

12
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:41,110
Three rules.

13
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,470
Code computes the facts, AI only writes the words.

14
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:46,330
A human always sends.

15
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:48,650
And tickets never depend on the AI.

16
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:57,190
Authors get a portal to sign in, see their books, raise tickets, and watch replies arrive live.

17
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:01,870
Staff get a queue with filters, status, notes, and assignment.

18
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:12,770
The AI sorts every ticket into BookLeaf's six categories, sets one of four priorities, and drafts a reply from the knowledge base.

19
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:15,870
Staff can override all of it.

20
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:24,670
Every ticket flows through a safe pipeline: triage, real data, an AI draft, then a checker that verifies every amount and date.

21
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:33,750
And I measured it: forty-five of forty-six test tickets classified right, Hindi and Hinglish included.

22
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:39,210
Even with the AI switched off, simple rules got eighty-seven percent.

23
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,630
Cost was a design choice.

24
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,030
A small, fast model sorts.

25
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,770
A stronger one only writes drafts.

26
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:52,970
Each prompt carries just the facts it needs, and admins can see the usage.

27
00:01:53,800 --> 00:02:01,610
And if the AI fails, keyword rules and template drafts take over, so the desk never stops.

28
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:07,750
Then the big idea: don't make authors leave their dashboard.

29
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:12,190
Support became a module that plugs into any product, for any company.

30
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:15,410
BookLeaf is just workspace number one.

31
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:22,690
Under the hood: one TypeScript monorepo, four apps, and one shared set of Zod schemas.

32
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,170
Fastify and Postgres with row-level security.

33
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:29,790
React and Tailwind.

34
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:31,930
Claude for triage and drafts.

35
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:34,210
OpenAI Realtime for voice.

36
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:36,990
Real databases in every test.

37
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:47,850
Everything runs through a versioned REST API, with sign-in, role-based access, validation on every request, and full Swagger docs.

38
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:57,330
Then I went beyond the brief: chat and voice in one thread, and calls that fill in the ticket while you talk.

39
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:00,110
It wasn't all smooth.

40
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:04,670
Vercel can't carry WebSockets, so calls get a one-time ticket to the API.

41
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:09,890
An audit found cross-company leaks, so I fixed them and added row-level security.

42
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:19,310
Quality was a habit: over twelve hundred automated tests, all green, on real databases.

43
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:25,670
It's live on AWS and Vercel.

44
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:33,130
It deploys only when I ask, backs up the database first, and one command tears it all down.

45
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:39,210
Next: a bigger real test set, one-click approvals, and email alerts.

46
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:45,990
From a support portal, to a support module any product can plug in.

47
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:47,230
Thanks for watching.
